  {
   "slug": "studio",
   "title": "Studio",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"className\":\"statement\"} --><p class=\"statement\">Archform is a studio of nine architects working from a former foundry in Westmere. We take on few projects at a time and see each one through to the last detail.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:model}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\",\"align\":\"wide\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image alignwide size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:model}}\" alt=\"A white study model\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:model}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Every project is tested in white card models before it is drawn in detail.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Principles</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Build less, and build it well.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use a small palette of honest materials.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Design for the light of the site, at every hour.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Make buildings that are easy to repair.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Practice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Services</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Feasibility, architecture, interiors, landscape coordination and site supervision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Team</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Nine architects, two model makers and a studio dog. We collaborate with the same engineers on most projects.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->",
   "template": "page-wide",
   "menu_order": 2
  },
  {
   "slug": "awards",
   "title": "Awards",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"className\":\"lead\"} --><p class=\"lead\">Prizes are not why we work, but they are a welcome sign that a building is doing its job.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"className\":\"awards is-style-left\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-table awards is-style-left\"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Award</th><th>Project</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026</td><td>Regional Architecture Prize, Residential</td><td>House on the Ridge</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Civic Trust Award</td><td>Kiln Lane Library</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Concrete Society, Commendation</td><td>Lakeside Pavilion</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Young Practice of the Year, shortlist</td><td>Studio</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Sacred Spaces Award</td><td>Chapel of Light</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Housing Design Award</td><td>Tower 9 Housing</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Publications</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><em>Quiet Concrete</em>, monograph, 2025</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Feature: Kiln Lane Library, quarterly architecture review, 2025</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Essay: \"On Thresholds\", studio journal, 2024</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->",
   "parent": "studio",
   "menu_order": 3
  },
  {
   "slug": "contact",
   "title": "Contact",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"className\":\"statement\"} --><p class=\"statement\">We take on four to six new commissions a year. Tell us about your site and your intentions; we reply to every enquiry personally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Studio</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Studio 4, Foundry Yard<br>18 Kiln Lane<br>Westmere</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Write</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"mailto:studio@archform.example\">studio@archform.example</a><br><a href=\"tel:+15550192200\">+1 (555) 019-2200</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->",
   "menu_order": 4
  }
 ],
 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "on-thresholds",
   "title": "On thresholds",
   "date": "2026-10-19T08:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "essays"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "light",
    "threshold"
   ],
   "featured_media": "arch",
   "excerpt": "The moment between outside and inside is the most important square metre of a building.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every building has a moment where the outside world stops and the inside begins. We call it the threshold, and we spend more time on it than on any other square metre of a project.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A good threshold slows you down. It might be a deep reveal in a thick wall, a change in floor material, a step, or simply a drop in light. It tells your body that something is about to change.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A door is a hinge in time as much as in space.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Studio notebook</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Three thresholds we return to</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>The deep reveal.</strong> A wall thick enough to stand inside.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>The low ceiling.</strong> A compressed entry before a tall room.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>The turn.</strong> A path that makes you face the light before you enter.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:courtyard}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\",\"align\":\"wide\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image alignwide size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:courtyard}}\" alt=\"Light falling into a courtyard\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:courtyard}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Chapel of Light: the threshold is a courtyard you cross in shade.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>None of these costs much. They cost attention, which is the only thing a small studio can promise in unlimited quantity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": true,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "M. Lindqvist",
     "email": "ml@example.com",
     "content": "Beautifully put. The compressed entry before the tall room is everything.",
     "date": "2026-10-20T07:44:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "house-on-the-ridge",
   "title": "House on the Ridge",
   "date": "2026-10-02T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "projects"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "concrete",
    "residential"
   ],
   "featured_media": "cantilever",
   "excerpt": "A single concrete bar, cantilevered six metres over the slope, frames one view and hides all the others.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The site was a steep ridge with a view in every direction. Our clients asked for all of them. We gave them one, framed by a single concrete bar that reaches six metres beyond the slope.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The plan</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bedrooms sit in the half anchored to the hill. The living room occupies the cantilever, with a single window the full width of the room. From the sofa you see sky and the far valley, never the neighbours.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Board-marked concrete, left untreated</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Oak floors and joinery</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One window, 11 metres wide</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ground-source heating and a green roof</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Choosing one view made the house feel bigger than showing them all.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Project architect</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "concrete-that-ages-well",
   "title": "Concrete that ages well",
   "date": "2026-09-18T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "essays",
    "materials"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "concrete"
   ],
   "featured_media": "concrete",
   "excerpt": "Board marks, tie holes and rain stains: designing concrete to look better in twenty years than on opening day.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Concrete has a reputation for ageing badly. Most of the time the material is not to blame; the detailing is. Water, more than time, decides how a concrete building looks in twenty years.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What we do</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Shape every top edge so rain drips clear of the façade.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use rough board-marked formwork so stains read as texture.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Set tie holes on a deliberate grid.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Avoid paint and sealers that peel.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The result is a surface that records the weather slowly and evenly, like stone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Design the stain before the rain designs it for you.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Studio maxim</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "R. Okoye",
     "email": "ro@example.com",
     "content": "The drip-edge point is underrated. Thank you for sharing your details.",
     "date": "2026-09-19T12:00:00"
    }
   ]
  },
