   "featured_media": "facade",
   "excerpt": "A neighbourhood library behind a façade of deep reveals, where the reading room is lit from above.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Kiln Lane Library sits on a busy street. We wanted it to feel open without exposing readers to the traffic. The answer was a façade of deep square reveals: transparent when you face it, almost solid when you pass at an angle.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Inside</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The reading room is lit by north-facing rooflights, so the light is even all day and never falls on a page. The children's library opens onto a small walled garden.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:beams}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:beams}}\" alt=\"Deep beams over the reading room\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:beams}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The reading room: deep beams soften the light from the rooflights.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A library should feel like a shelter from the street, not a shop window.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Project architect</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The library opened in 2025 and now lends more books than any other branch in the city.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "drawing-by-hand",
   "title": "Why we still draw by hand",
   "date": "2026-08-21T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "essays",
    "studio"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "process"
   ],
   "featured_media": "sketch",
   "excerpt": "Every project starts with a soft pencil and tracing paper. Here is why we have not given it up.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our studio runs on the same software as everyone else. But every project still begins on tracing paper, with a soft pencil and a scale ruler.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A hand drawing is honest about its uncertainty. A wobbly line invites a question; a crisp digital line looks finished long before it is.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the pencil is good for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Testing many ideas quickly, without committing to any.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Drawing light and shadow, which are hard to fake on screen.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Talking with clients across a table rather than a monitor.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The computer is for being right. The pencil is for being curious.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Studio partner</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "lakeside-pavilion",
   "title": "Lakeside Pavilion",
   "date": "2026-08-07T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "projects"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "landscape"
   ],
   "featured_media": "pavilion",
   "excerpt": "A roof plane eleven centimetres thick, eight slender columns and nothing else.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The pavilion is a shelter for walkers on the lake path. It is a single roof plane, eleven centimetres thick, resting on eight steel columns. There are no walls.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We spent most of the design time on the edge of the roof. It had to be thin enough to disappear against the water, and stiff enough to span twelve metres.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Facts</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Span: 12 m</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Roof: post-tensioned concrete, 110 mm</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Columns: solid steel, 90 mm diameter</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cost: less than a single family car park</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On still mornings the underside of the roof reflects the water, and the pavilion seems to float.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "J. Aalto-Price",
     "email": "jap@example.com",
     "content": "Walked under it last weekend. The thin edge really does disappear against the lake.",
     "date": "2026-08-10T11:05:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "the-white-model",
   "title": "The white model",
   "date": "2026-07-24T09:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "studio"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "process"
   ],
   "featured_media": "model",
   "excerpt": "Before colour, before material, a building has to work as pure form. Our card models test exactly that.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every project in the studio is built at least once as a white card model. No colour, no texture, no people: just volumes, openings and light.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>White models are cruel. A weak proportion has nowhere to hide. If a building works in white card under a desk lamp, it will usually work in stone in the sun.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Our rules for models</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Same scale for every option, so they can be compared.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Photograph each one at the site's sunrise angle.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Never throw one away until the building is finished.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The model is the first building. The real one is the second attempt.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Model maker</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "stairs-as-rooms",
   "title": "Stairs as rooms",
   "date": "2026-07-09T08:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "essays"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "light"
   ],
   "featured_media": "stair",
   "excerpt": "A stair can be the most generous space in a building, if it is given light and room to pause.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In most buildings the stair is a shaft hidden behind a fire door. In ours it is often the best room in the house.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A stair is where you move between worlds: from the street to the bedroom, from work to rest. Given width, a window and a landing to stop on, it becomes a place rather than a route.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What makes a good stair</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A gentle pitch: low risers and deep treads.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Daylight at the top, drawing you up.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A landing wide enough for a chair.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:stair}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:stair}}\" alt=\"A concrete stair rising to the light\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:stair}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Foundry Yard Studios: the stair is lit from a rooflight at the top.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When a client asks where to save money, we rarely suggest the stair.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  }
